            <ArticleTitle>HIV infection in surgical practice.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>264-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>When HIV is mentioned in a surgical forum a debate will often follow about &quot;universal testing&quot; and &quot;barrier precautions&quot;. Unfortunately, there is little discussion of the many ways that HIV-infected patients may present primarily to the surgeon. Although lifestyle risk factors are important clues, unusual clinical features of a relatively common surgical disease should raise the suspicion of HIV infection.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
